// moarchy-store can install nothing without this file, and the reason is the
// image's own doing: vm/configure.sh runs `passwd -l` on the account, because
// tty1 autologin never consults a password and a VM on a forwarded port should
// not have a known one. The store's polkit action asks for that password anyway
// (`auth_self_keep` in org.moarchy.Store.policy), so pkexec can never be
// authenticated and every app in the catalogue fails to install.
//
// So grant the action outright, as moarchy's own copy of this rule does. Three
// things bound what that gives away:
//
//   * The action does not point at pacman. It points at
//     /usr/lib/moarchy-store/moarchy-store-helper, which refuses any package not
//     in the root-owned catalogue -- or in a published one that gpgv verifies
//     against the shipped keyring and whose serial is not older. This is
//     "install one of these curated apps", not "install anything".
//   * The same user already holds `NOPASSWD: ALL` from
//     /etc/sudoers.d/wheel-nopasswd. Everything reachable through this action is
//     reachable more directly through sudo, so this is the narrower of two
//     doors that are already open, not a new one.
//   * wheel, not everyone. On this image that group is the single autologin
//     user.
//
// A rule here rather than <allow_active>yes</allow_active> in the store's own
// .policy: "this image has no password" is the image's decision, and the store
// is a separate component that has to stay honest elsewhere.
//
// Deliberately not gated on subject.local && subject.active. A shell restarted
// over ssh lands in a session with no seat, and an active-only rule would put
// the password dialog back where it cannot be answered.
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
  if (action.id === "org.moarchy.store.manage" && subject.isInGroup("wheel")) {
    return polkit.Result.YES;
  }
});
